>> title: Jalfrezi
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: chicken, indian, peppers, stir-fry, curry
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: jhal farezi, jal frezi, chicken jalfrezi, jalfrazi, jalfreezi
>> pairs-with: onion-tomato-masala, pilau-rice, chapati
>> servings: 4
>> time: 40 min
>> step.1: sear 5 min over the highest heat, then lift out
>> step.2: fry 3 min, until scorched in places and still crisp
>> step.3: fry in 2 min
>> step.4: toss back in, 2 min, and stop

Sear @boneless chicken thighs{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; cut in strips) salted with @kosher salt{1%tsp} in @neutral oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL) in a #wide pan{} over the highest heat you have ~sear{5%min}, then lift the strips out.

Fry @bell peppers{2}(one red, one green; cut in 1-in squares) and @red onion{1%medium}(cut in eighths, layers separated) in the same pan ~fry{3%min}, until they are scorched in places and still squeak against a tooth.

Fry @onion-tomato masala{1%cup}(240 g; the base recipe), @ground cumin{1%tsp}, @ground turmeric{1/2%tsp} and @garam masala{1%tsp} into @&(~1)peppers{} ~fry{2%min}.

Toss @&(~3)seared chicken{} back into @&(~1)pan{} with @tomato{1%large}(cut in eighths), @green chiles{2}(slit lengthwise), @white vinegar{2%tsp}(10 mL) and @cilantro{3%Tbs}(chopped) ~fry{2%min}, and take it off while the peppers are still bright.

This is the one line on the sauce list that is a stir-fry and not a simmer, and the vinegar at the end is why it tastes sharper than everything either side of it on the board. Jhal frezi is a Bengali name for frying up cold meat from the day before, which is exactly what it is — the base does the work of a sauce in two minutes, and anything longer than that steams the peppers and loses the dish.
